-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/7aa9bdfc-6371-49b0-bcf7-f146622d31d5
-- title:
--   Theorem 3.1, p.14 — learning in Ellsberg's urns: rejected iff ambiguity is large, otherwise sample until $|Z|$ hits $\overline z$ and bet on the ambiguous urn (corrected: (19))
-- statement:
--   Let $0<\alpha<\tfrac12$, $0<\epsilon<1$, $\sigma>0$, $c>0$, and take any valid signal model for $\sigma$ with the Ellsberg specification (17)–(19): the bias towards red is $\pm\alpha$, the priors give $+\alpha$ probability in $[\frac{1-\epsilon}{2},\frac{1+\epsilon}{2}]$, and the bets pay $\tfrac12\pm\alpha$ (ambiguous urn) or $\tfrac12$ (risky urn). With $l(r)=2\log\frac{r}{1-r}-\frac1r+\frac1{1-r}$, the equation $l(\hat r)=\frac{2\alpha^3}{c\sigma^2}$ (22) has a unique root $\hat r\in(0,1)$, and:
--
--   1. $\tfrac12<\hat r$; the set $\mathcal P_0$ of predictive priors is nonempty; and $X_0=X(0,0)=\tfrac12$.
--   2. **(i)** Stopping immediately ($\tau^*=0$) is optimal in $\max_{\tau\in\Gamma}\min_{P\in\mathcal P_0}E_P(X_\tau-c\tau)$ if and only if $\frac{1+\epsilon}{2}\ge\hat r$.
--   3. **(ii)** If $\frac{1+\epsilon}{2}<\hat r$, then $l(r)+l(\frac{1+\epsilon}{2})=\frac{4\alpha^3}{c\sigma^2}$ (24) has a unique root $\bar r\in(0,1)$, $\hat r<\bar r$, and with
--   $$\overline z=\frac{\sigma^2}{2\alpha}\Big[\log\frac{1+\epsilon}{1-\epsilon}+\log\frac{\bar r}{1-\bar r}\Big]>0\quad(23):$$
--      - $\tau^*=\min\{t\ge0:|Z_t|\ge\overline z\}$ is optimal and $\tau^*>0$;
--      - on stopping, if $Z_{\tau^*}\ge\overline z$ the bet on red is strictly better than the bet on blue and the risky urn, if $Z_{\tau^*}\le-\overline z$ the bet on blue is, and the risky urn is never optimal ($u_2<X_{\tau^*}$);
--      - if $\epsilon<\epsilon'<2\hat r-1$ and $\tau^{*\prime}$ is the corresponding stopping time (for $\epsilon'$, in the same signal model), then $\tau^{*\prime}\ge\tau^*$.
--
--   Learning is rejected exactly when ambiguity is large relative to the stakes; when some learning is optimal, the risky urn is chosen only if no learning takes place, and more ambiguity means longer sampling.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: (19): $u(a_0,\alpha)=u(a_1,-\alpha)=\alpha-\tfrac12$ → $u(a_0,\alpha)=u(a_1,-\alpha)=\tfrac12-\alpha$; reason: the printed value is negative for every $0<\alpha<1/2$, against the nonnegativity of payoffs assumed on p.11; the paper's (20) (p.13), $u_2=\tfrac12(u_{00}+u_{10})$ (p.19) and $u_{11}-u_{10}=2\alpha$ used in the proof (p.31) all require $\tfrac12-\alpha$ (prizes 1 and 0)
--   - generalization: the augmented filtration $\{\mathcal G_t\}$ generated by $Z$ → any filtration $\mathbb G$ to which $Z$ is adapted and under which $Z/\sigma$ is a $(\mathbb G,P_0)$-Brownian motion; reason: the paper's filtration, with an augmentation that keeps its p.6 assertion true, is one of them, so the statement specializes to it; stopping times and density generators range over the larger class
--   - encoding: $P^\eta$ given by its density $dP^\eta/dP_0|_{\mathcal G_t}=\exp\{-\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds-\int_0^t\eta_sdW_s\}$ (printed without the factor $\tfrac12$) → the priors $Q$ with a density generator $\eta\in\Xi$, equivalent to $P_0$ on every $\mathbb G_t$, under which $W^\eta=Z/\sigma+\int_0^\cdot\eta_sds$ is a $(\mathbb G,Q)$-Brownian motion; reason: Girsanov characterization of $P^\eta$, since Mathlib has no Itô integral; for the paper's filtration it determines $Q$ on every $\mathcal G_t$, which is all the objective (9) depends on
--   - reading: §2: "each $\eta\in\Xi$ defines a probability measure ... $P^\eta$" (by Girsanov's theorem) → a standing property of the signal model: every bounded progressively measurable $\eta$ yields such a $Q$; reason: the paper asserts this as a consequence of Girsanov's theorem, which Mathlib lacks; it holds on the canonical space of continuous paths with Wiener measure and the natural filtration (the worst-case-prior milestone asserts that a valid model exists), and it fails on a filtration augmented by all null sets of $\mathbb G_\infty$, on which $P^\eta$ is singular to $P_0$
--   - encoding: $\eta_t(\omega)\in\Xi_t(\omega)$ for $dt\otimes dP_0$-a.e. $(t,\omega)$ → for every $(t,\omega)$; reason: clamping an a.e. selection to the interval $\Xi_t(\omega)$ gives a progressively measurable selection everywhere with the same $P^\eta$; the set of priors is unchanged
--   - encoding: posteriors $\underline m_t,\overline m_t$ by Bayesian updating (Liptser–Shiryaev, Theorem 9.1) → the closed form (12)–(13), as a function of $(t,z)$; reason: the paper's own formula; same objects
--   - encoding: stopping times with values in $\mathbb R_+$ → stopping times in $[0,\infty]$ (`WithTop ℝ≥0`); on $\{\tau=\infty\}$ the payoff is evaluated at time $0$; reason: for $\tau\in\Gamma$ the set $\{\tau=\infty\}$ is null under every prior, so the objective is unchanged
--   - encoding: $\min\{t\ge0:\dots\}$ → the first hitting time `hittingAfter` of a closed set, $=\infty$ if it is never hit; reason: with continuous paths the minimum is attained when the set is hit; paths that never hit are null under every prior for the stopping times of the paper
--   - reading: "the optimal stopping time $\tau^*$ is given by ..." → the given $\tau^*$ is optimal in (9); reason: the proofs establish exactly this ("$\tau^*$ is the optimal stopping time", p.28); uniqueness is neither claimed nor proved
--   - reading: (i) "$\tau^*=0$ if and only if $(1+\epsilon)/2\ge\hat r$" → immediate stopping $\tau\equiv0$ is optimal iff $(1+\epsilon)/2\ge\hat r$; reason: the page's gloss ("it is optimal to reject learning if and only if ambiguity ... is suitably large", p.14); this is the stronger reading
--   - reading: "the bet on red is chosen (if $Z_{\tau^*}\ge\overline z$)" → its Gilboa–Schmeidler utility strictly exceeds those of the bet on blue and of the risky urn; reason: "chosen" means it is the optimal action; the strict form implies the weak one
--   - generalization: $\mathcal P_0$ nonempty (presupposed: $P^*\in\mathcal P_0$) → asserted as a conclusion; reason: an extra conclusion; it rules out an empty set of priors, on which the objective would be degenerate
--   - generalization: the text before the theorem: $\hat r$ is uniquely defined by (22) and $\tfrac12<\hat r<1$ → part of the conclusion; reason: an extra conclusion, stated on p.14 just before Theorem 3.1
--   - encoding: "$\tau^{*\prime}$ is the corresponding optimal stopping time" → the first hitting time of $\{|Z|\ge\overline z(\epsilon',\bar r')\}$ in the same signal model; reason: by part (ii) at $\epsilon'$ it is the optimal stopping time for $\epsilon'$
-- source:
--   Epstein & Ji, Optimal Learning under Robustness and Time-Consistency, arXiv:1708.01890v2, Theorem 3.1, p.14; (21)–(22), p.13

import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_Model
import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_CriticalValues
import Definitions.Def_EllsbergLearning_Ellsberg

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Set
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace EllsbergLearning

/-- Theorem 3.1 (Epstein & Ji, *Optimal Learning under Robustness and Time-Consistency*,
arXiv:1708.01890v2, p.14), learning in Ellsberg's urns. Let `0 < α < 1/2`, `0 < ϵ < 1`, `σ > 0`,
`c > 0`, and take any signal model (`Ω`, `P₀`, `𝔾`, `Z`) satisfying the standing assumptions of §2
for the volatility `σ`, with the Ellsberg specification (17)–(19) (payoff `1/2 − α` in (19)). Then
(22) has a unique root `r̂ ∈ (0, 1)`, and for it:

* `1/2 < r̂`; the set `P0` of predictive priors is nonempty; `X₀ = X(0, 0) = 1/2`;
* (i) stopping immediately (`τ* = 0`) is optimal in (9) iff `(1 + ϵ)/2 ≥ r̂`;
* (ii) if `(1 + ϵ)/2 < r̂`: (24) has a unique root `r̄ ∈ (0, 1)`; `r̂ < r̄`; `z̄ > 0` with `z̄` of
  (23); `τ* = min{t ≥ 0 : |Z_t| ≥ z̄}` is optimal in (9) and `τ* > 0`; on stopping
  (`τ* = t < ∞`), if `Z_{τ*} ≥ z̄` the bet on red `a₁` is strictly better than both other bets,
  if `Z_{τ*} ≤ −z̄` the bet on blue `a₀` is, and the bet on the risky urn `a₂` is never optimal
  (`u₂ < X_{τ*}`); finally, if `ϵ < ϵ' < 2r̂ − 1` and `τ*'` is the corresponding stopping time
  (for `ϵ'` and its root `r̄'` of (24), same model), then `τ*' ≥ τ*`.

**Formalization Note.** "The optimal stopping time ... is given by `τ*`" is read as "`τ*` is
optimal"; "(i) `τ* = 0` iff ..." as "immediate stopping is optimal iff ...". The stopping time is
the first hitting time `hittingAfter` of `{|Z| ≥ z̄}`, with values in `[0, ∞]`. -/
theorem ellsberg_optimal_learning (α ϵ σ c : ℝ) (hα : 0 < α) (hα' : α < 1 / 2) (hϵ : 0 < ϵ)
    (hϵ' : ϵ < 1) (hσ : 0 < σ) (hc : 0 < c) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (M : SignalModel Ω) (hM : M.IsValid σ) :
    let p := ellsberg α ϵ σ c
    let hit : ℝ → Ω → WithTop ℝ≥0 := fun z => hittingAfter (fun t ω => |M.Z t ω|) (Ici z) 0
    (∃! rhat : ℝ, rhat ∈ Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1 ∧ ell rhat = 2 * α ^ 3 / (c * σ ^ 2)) ∧
    ∀ rhat : ℝ, rhat ∈ Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1 → ell rhat = 2 * α ^ 3 / (c * σ ^ 2) →
      1 / 2 < rhat ∧ (M.priors p).Nonempty ∧ p.X 0 0 = 1 / 2 ∧
      (M.IsOptimal p (fun _ => 0) ↔ rhat ≤ (1 + ϵ) / 2) ∧
      ((1 + ϵ) / 2 < rhat →
        (∃! rbar : ℝ, rbar ∈ Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1 ∧
          ell rbar + ell ((1 + ϵ) / 2) = 4 * α ^ 3 / (c * σ ^ 2)) ∧
        ∀ rbar : ℝ, rbar ∈ Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1 →
          ell rbar + ell ((1 + ϵ) / 2) = 4 * α ^ 3 / (c * σ ^ 2) →
          rhat < rbar ∧ 0 < zbar α ϵ σ rbar ∧
          M.IsOptimal p (hit (zbar α ϵ σ rbar)) ∧
          (∀ ω, 0 < hit (zbar α ϵ σ rbar) ω) ∧
          (∀ (ω : Ω) (t : ℝ≥0), hit (zbar α ϵ σ rbar) ω = t →
            (zbar α ϵ σ rbar ≤ M.Z t ω →
              p.gs 0 t (M.Z t ω) < p.gs 1 t (M.Z t ω) ∧ p.u₂ < p.gs 1 t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
            (M.Z t ω ≤ -zbar α ϵ σ rbar →
              p.gs 1 t (M.Z t ω) < p.gs 0 t (M.Z t ω) ∧ p.u₂ < p.gs 0 t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
            p.u₂ < p.X t (M.Z t ω)) ∧
          ∀ ϵ'' rbar'' : ℝ, ϵ < ϵ'' → ϵ'' < 2 * rhat - 1 → rbar'' ∈ Ioo (0 : ℝ) 1 →
            ell rbar'' + ell ((1 + ϵ'') / 2) = 4 * α ^ 3 / (c * σ ^ 2) →
            ∀ ω, hit (zbar α ϵ σ rbar) ω ≤ hit (zbar α ϵ'' σ rbar'') ω) := by sorry

end EllsbergLearning
